## 1. Introduction
Humidity sensors have attracted more and more attention due to their wide application in industry [1,2], agriculture [3,4], environmental protection [5] and personal life [6–8]. In the past decades, different materials were utilized to prepare humidity sensors, such as carbon materials [9], semiconductor metal oxides [10], perovskite [11], polymer [12,13] and so on. However, it’s not easy for humidity sensors to satisfy easy preparation, high sensitivity, high stability, low hysteresis and fast response. For example, hydrophilic polymers have the advantages of easy solution process, but they suffer from poor stability under high humidity atmosphere. Sensors based on metal oxides show superior sensing properties and thermal stability, but they require external heating and complicated operation [14,15]. Therefore, rational design and synthesis of sensing materials are urgently needed for high performance humidity sensors.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs), a class of materials with high specific surface area and controllable structure, have been widely used in drug delivery [16], chemical catalysis [17], sensors [18,19] and gas separation [20]. The precise structure, persistent porosity and modifiable structure of MOFs offer a platform to construct porous electrolytes and processing into sensitive film through chemical interactions. These advantages make MOFs attractive for humidity sensors. Some MOFs have been reported as the sensing materials of humidity sensors [21–23]. These MOFs show good long-term stability, but their poor film-forming ability and processability restrict their practical application.
In this work, polyelectrolyte humidity sensors based on UIO-66 derived polyelectrolyte film were prepared. The introduction of MOFs unit could ensure the stability of the sensitive films in high-humidity atmosphere and provide hydrophilicity of the polymers. Benefit from the inherent pores, structure defects, proper hydrophilicity and structural modification of MOFs materials, humidity sensors with short response and recovery times $(3.1\ s/1.5\ s)$ were obtained. The humidity sensing mechanism was studied systematically through complex impedance spectroscopy (CIS) with Z-View and equivalent circuit (EC). The QCM devices were also used to explain the water adsorption with different loading amount. The obtained sensor was used for breath state monitoring, which can distinguish different breath states well.
## 2. Experiment section
## 2.1. Materials
All chemicals used in this work are shown in supporting information.
## 2.2. Preparation of UIO-66-Sx- ${\cdot}N H_{2}$
$\mathsf{U I O-}66{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{S X-N H_{2}}$ with different molar ratio $(\mathbf{x}(0,1,2,3,4)$ stands for $2{\cdot}S0_{3}\mathrm{Na}$ -BDC: $0\%$ , $10\%$ , $20\%$ , $30\%$ , $40\%$ , respectively) were synthesized according to the reported method with a little revision [24]. Typically, for UIO- ${\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{S}1{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , $466~\mathrm{{mg}~\ z r{C l_{4}}}$ (2 mmol), $326\mathrm{mg}$ 2-aminoterephthalic acid $\cdot1.8\mathrm{mmol}\rangle$ , $53\mathrm{mg}$ sodium 2- sulfonate terephthalic acid $(0.2\mathrm{mmol})\mathrm{,}$ were dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF, then $3{\mathrm{mL}}$ of hydrochloric acid and $3{\mathrm{mL}}$ of acetic acid were added after ultrasonic treatment for $30\mathrm{min}$ . The mixture was then transferred into the Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave followed by heating at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . After cooling to room temperature, yellow solid was collected after washed with DMF and ethanol 3 times, finally, $600\mathrm{mg}$ yellow powder was obtained after drying at $50^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{{h}}}$ under reduced pressure. Materials with other ratios were synthesized with a similar process. The actual mole ratio of $S/Z\mathrm{r}$ is shown in Table S1.
## 2.3. Preparation of UIO-66-Sx-NH-AA
UIO-66-S1-NH-AA was obtained according to the reported method [25]. Typically, $117\mathrm{\mg\UIO{-}\it{66-}S1{-}N H_{2}}$ (0.4 mmol $-\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ ) was suspended in $4\mathrm{mL}$ of chloroform, treated with two equivalents of acrylic anhydride, heated and stirred at $55^{\circ}C$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Yellow solid was collected after centrifugation and washed with ethanol, finally, $100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ yellow powder was obtained after drying at $50^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ under reduced pressure. Materials with others ratios were synthesized with a similar process.
## 2.4. Preparation of humidity sensors
According to the ratio of $2{\cdot}S0_{3}\mathsf{N a}$ -BDC (0, 10, 20, 30, $40\%$ ), the corresponding humidity sensors were marked as P0, P1, P2, P3, P4. Take P1 sensor as an example, $13.68\mathrm{mg}$ UIO-66-S1-NH-AA $\left(0.04\mathrm{mmol}\right.$ $-C\mathrm{H}{=}\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ ), $4.89\mathrm{mg}$ pentaerythritol tetra(3- mercaptopropionate) (PETMP, 0.01 mmol $-S\mathrm{H})$ and $1\mathrm{mg}$ benzoin dimethyl ether (catalyst) were dissolved in $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ THF. Then, $6{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ well prepared solution was drop-cast onto a ceramic plate with four pairs of interdigitated carbon electrodes. The substrates were then irradiated under UV light for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Finally, rinse the humidity sensors with THF to remove the unreacted PETMP and the catalyst. The P0, P2, P3, P4 sensors were prepared with the same method by changing the amount of UIO-66-Sx-NH-AA (Scheme 1).
## 2.5. Preparation of QCM sensors
QCM sensors were prepared under following steps. The solutions for prepare P0, P1, P2, P3, P4 were diluted 10 times, then $2\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the solution was drop-cast to QCM component. Then the substrates were transferred and irradiated under UV light. After rinsing with THF, QCM sensors were well prepared and used for detecting the water adsorption and desorption.
## 2.6. Measurements
Measurements and testing methods are shown in supporting information.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Materials characterization
Fig. 1a shows that the XRD patterns of UIO- $66{\cdot}\mathsf{S x}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ . They are all well agree to the simulated UIO-66 XRD pattern, demonstrating the structure of $\mathrm{UIO-}66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Sx-NH_{2}}$ were similar to UIO-66. Meanwhile, half-width of the XRD patterns of UIO $\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\left.-66-53-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\aftergroup\egroup\right.$ and UIO-66-S4- ${\cdot\}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ becomes wider and the intensity decreases, suggesting the ligand change from 2-aminoterephthalic acid to sodium 2-sulfonate terephthalic acid cause the deterioration of MOFs structure [26]. The XRD patterns of UIO-66-Sx-NH-AA are in good agreement of UIO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , which indicates that the crystal structure of UIO- $66{\-}\mathrm{Sx}{\-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was well maintained after modification by acrylic anhydride (Fig. 1b).
To determine whether MOFs have been well modified and then cross linked with PETMP, FT-IR were carried out. As shown in Fig. 2a, the characteristic peak at 1022, 1078, $1100\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be seen as the existence of $\scriptstyle0=5=0$ [27], and the peaks of ANHCOA appears at 1640 and $1680\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in UIO-66-Sx-NH-AA, which proves the success of modification (Fig. 2b) [28]. The peaks at 670, 1236 and $1420\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are considered to be the vibration peaks of $-S{\mathrm{-}}C{\mathrm{H}}_{2}- $ represents the success of the click reaction (Fig. 2c) [29]. The TGA curves show that the polymers start to collapse when the temperature raised to $300^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The weight change between $300^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and $400^{\circ}\mathsf C$ was considered to be the damage of polymer matrix $\left(-S{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{H}_{2}-\mathbf{\right.}$ between PETMP and UIO-66-Sx-NHAA) [30] and the weight loss from $400^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to $550^{\circ}\mathsf C$ was the landslide of MOFs [26], These data demonstrate the obtained polymers own good thermal stability (Fig. S1).
The morphologies of the polyelectrolyte films were investigated by SEM and HRTEM. As seen in Figs. 3, 4 and Figs. S2, S3, UIO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ and UIO- $66{\cdot}\mathsf{S3-N H}_{2}$ shows spherical agglomeration, with the adding of different sodium 2-sulfonate terephthalic acid, the morphology does not show obvious change. However, through HRTEM images, the morphology of $\mathsf{U I O-}66{-}\mathsf{S}3{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ and UIO-66-S4- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ shows obvious change to UIO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , which may due to the deterioration of MOFs structure (Fig. 3a, 3d, 4a, 4d and Fig. S2a, 2d, $2\mathrm{g}$ S4a, 4c, 4e). After modification by acrylic anhydride, the morphologies did not change, indicating that the structure of UIO-66-Sx- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was well maintained after modification (Fig. 3b, 3e, 4b, 4d and
Fig. S2b, 2e, 2h, S4b, 4d, 4f). For polyelectrolyte films, P0 and P3 both show homogeneously dispersed $\mathsf{J I O-66-S x-N H_{2}}$ particles in the polymer with no agglomeration, P1, P2 and P4 show similar results (Fig. S2c, 2f, and 2i). Besides, the particles were surrounded by the polymer, indicating that the existence of ${\mathsf{-S-C H}}_{2}.$ - between particles and polymer can effectively enhance the adhesion and eliminated interfacial defects (Fig. 3c, 3f) [30].
The surface area and pore size of UI $\mathsf{\Gamma}){-}66{-}\mathsf{S x{-}N H_{2}}$ determined though $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption were collected and shown in Fig S4. As the increase of $2{\cdot}\mathsf{S O}_{3}\mathsf{N a}$ -BDC, the surface area become smaller (from 917 to $422\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ with I type isotherms, the pore size is about $0.7{\mathrm{nm}}$ . The decrease of surface area may be caused by the introduction of $2{\cdot}S0_{3}\mathrm{Na}$ -BDC. From the BET data and XRD patterns, it can be inferred that S3 and S4 contains many structure defects (such as linker-miss, partial coordination) [26].
## 3.2. Humidity sensing properties
The humidity sensing properties of P0, P1, P2, P3, P4 sensors at different relative humidities (RHs) were tested. As shown in Fig. 5a, P0 sensor shows a tiny change between $11\%-95\%$ RH which means P0 was not sensitive to humidity. P1, P2, P3, P4 sensors show different degrees of change in impedance when RH increases from $11\%$ to $95\%$ . The impedance changes of P3 sensor and P4 sensor were nearly two orders from $33\%$ to $95\%$ RH. Besides, since UIO66 has a small hysteresis due to the specific pore size of the frame itself [31]. The humidity hysteresis of P3 sensor is ${\sim}1.2\%$ RH, the humidity hysteresis of P1, P2 and P4 sensors are ${\sim}1.9\%$ , ${\sim}1.4\%$ and ${\sim}2.9\%$ RH, respectively (Fig. S5). Thus, P3 sensor was selected for the others tests, P3 sensor’s response at different frequencies was also researched. The linearities of the curves from 54 to $95\%$ RH were calculated $\mathrm{'R}^{2}=0.994)$ , and the best working frequency was selected as $1\mathrm{kHz}$ (Fig. 5b).
Response and recovery times are important parameters for humidity sensor, for P1, P2, P3, and P4 sensors, the response times and recovery times were measured and shown in Fig. 6. The response and recovery times were equal to the $90\%$ of impedance changes after the sensor was placed in the atmosphere of $33\%$ RH and $95\%$ RH respectively. The UIO-66 derived polyelectrolyte films contain sulfonate group and structural defects, which allow water molecules can be adsorbed and desorbed easily, so the humidity sensor show fast response to humidity change. P3 sensor’s response/recovery times are $3.1/1.5s.$ . For P1, P2, P4 sensors, the response/recovery times are 6.2/3.6 s, 4.8/3.1 s and $0.9/9.7s$ respectively. Compared to previously reported MOFs based humidity sensors (Table 1), our sensors show lower humidity hysteresis and much shorter response and recovery times.
## 3.3. Repeatability and stability
The key factors of real application for humidity sensor are repeatability and stability. The continuous response and recovery curves were measured and shown in Fig. 7a. The changeless baseline (impedance modulus in $33\%$ , $54\%$ , $75\%$ , $85\%$ and $95\%$ RH) and the maintenance of response and recovery times indicate the good repeatability of P3 sensor. To investigate the stability of P3 sensor, the impedance changes of P3 sensor at different RHs were monitored for 30 days and shown in Fig. 7b. Besides, due to the good water stability of UIO-66 derivatives, P3 sensor can return to the baseline quickly after being placed in $95\%$ RH for over $7000s$ (Fig. S6), which demonstrates the P3 sensor possesses excellent stability.
## 3.4. Sensing mechanism
To understanding the reason for fast response and small humidity hysteresis of P3 sensor, CIS (complex impedance spectroscopy) and EC (equivalent circuit) are shown in Fig. S7 and S8. At $11-33\%$ RH, few water molecules can be adsorbed to the channel and surface of MOFs film and thus the impedance modulus shows a tiny change, thus the CIS curves shows a line that parallel to the X-axis (Fig. S7), which can be viewed as a single CPE (Fig. S8a and b). With the increase of humidity, more water molecules are adsorbed by MOFs film though introduced sulfonate and pore but they are still too few. Thus, the ions conductivity of P3 is low under $54\%$ RH, the CIS curves are shown as a semicircle (phase angle is from $-10^{\circ}$ to $-90^{\circ}$ in Figs. S7 and S8c), and the EC can be described as ${\tt R}_{\mathrm{f}}$ and CPE in parallel in series [40]. At $75\text{\textperthousand}$ RH, water molecules are adsorbed into the MOFs channel and continuous water layers are formed in the MOFs film and enough to form ion transport, so the impedance modulus decreases with the increase of RH [41]. The CIS curves are shown as a semicircle and a single line, and the EC can be described as $\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{f}},$ CPE and $Z_{\mathrm{w}}$ in parallel and Rf in series (Fig. S8d, e and f). For comparison, complex impedance spectroscopy of P0 sensor was also measured. At $11-85\%$ RH, the CIS curves shows lines that parallel to the $\mathsf{X}$ -axis, until the RH reach to $95\%$ , the CIS curves are composed of a semicircle which shows that the adding of $2{-}\mathsf{S O}_{3}\mathsf{N a}{-}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ can really enhance the sensor conductivity (Fig. S9).
To further investigate the sensing mechanism, water adsorption capacity based QCM sensors were measured and shown in Fig. 8. P1 has the higher adsorption capacity than P0 for the increase of sulfonate in the framework and also due to the low content of sulfonate, water desorption for P1 is easy. P2 and P3 shows gradual decrease in water adsorption which should be caused by defects and destruction in the frame structure of MOFs with the content of sulfonic acid groups increases [26]. P4 shows a higher water adsorption ability than P3, which is attributed to the highest amount of sulfonate in P4 among all five humidity sensors, and so the P4 sensor exhibit a longer recovery time. During the adsorption process, the sulfonic acid groups have a synergistic effect with the MOFs’ own channels, which allows water molecules to be adsorbed on the surface and pores of the polymer film, and because of the continuous film structure, ions can be better transported to make the electrical signal of the sensor change rapidly [42], so the sensor owns a fast response property. When desorption occurs, the specific pore size of UIO-66 derivative and the defect of the frame makes the desorption process easier and more thorough [43].
Small water adsorption and physical desorption are the reason for ultrafast impedance response and recovery speed of P3 sensor, besides, defects caused by missing-linker/partial coordination allow water molecules to achieve fast adsorption and desorption though hydrogen bonds [43], so the sensor exhibits a small humidity hysteresis and fast response/recovery to humidity change than any other MOF based humidity sensors (Fig. 9).
## 3.5. Breath monitoring
Generally speaking, adults breathe 16–20 times per minute which means one breath cycle lasts $3{-}4s$ . It is a challenge for humidity sensors to realize a cycle in $4s$ . Because of the fast response and recovery time of P3 sensor, it was applied to record human breath monitoring. The subject exhibited four different breath states in 200 s for normal breath (Fig. S10a), fast breath (Fig. S10b), slow breath (Fig. S10c) and random breath (Fig. S10d), and these four breath states were recorded by P3 sensor. The P3 sensor can distinguish these four breath states. Due to the highly sensitive of P3 sensor, the impedance changes obviously during breath. As shown in Fig. 10 and Fig. S10, there is a platform in every cycle between inhaled and exhaled process, which is very important and helpful to identify single exhale and inhale. P3 sensor shows potential applications in personal state monitoring and breath analysis in medicine.
## 4. Conclusions
In conclusion, humidity sensors based on UIO-66 derived polyelectrolyte films were prepared by in situ thiol-ene click crosslinking polymerization in this work. Due to the inherent pore size of UIO-66 and the structural defects caused by the introduction of sulfonic acid, water molecules can achieve rapid adsorption and desorption, shows short response and recovery time $(3.1\ s/1.5s)$ , small humidity hysteresis $(\sim1.2\%$ RH), high humidity stability as well as convenient preparation. Mechanistic investigation and QCM analysis revealed that the origin of fast adsorption and desorption in the sensor. Real-time breath monitoring was successfully realized by the prepared sensor. Our research shows a new strategy for the development of continuous polyelectrolyte MOFs film in electronic sensors.
